window.KV_BOOKS={"oprisk-vol1": {"t": "Operational Risk in Banking", "s": "Theorems & Modeling · Volume 1", "d": "Fraud, cyber attacks, mis-selling, system outages, processing errors, rogue traders, failed vendors and floods: operational losses are rare, heavy-tailed and dependent, and they cost banks more each decade. This book treats them with the full toolkit of probability, statistics and operations research. Every classical model, from Panjer’s recursion and the loss distribution approach to extreme value theory, vine copulas, Bayesian credibility, Hawkes processes, inspection games and the Basel III standardized measurement approach, is stated, proved and credited to its authors. Each classical model is then extended. The KV_ models relax restrictive assumptions or add the constraints that practice demands, always containing the classical model as a special case, and every one ends in a number a risk manager can act on: a capital figure, a control budget, a key risk indicator threshold or a decision. Throughout, the methods are applied to Waltair Bank, a hypothetical bank with a complete synthetic loss history.", "kw": "Operational risk · LDA · EVT · Vine copulas · Hawkes · SMA capital · KRIs"}, "ai-oprisk-vol2": {"t": "Artificial Intelligence for Operational Risk in Banking", "s": "Volume 2", "d": "An AI model earns its place in operational risk only if it makes a key risk indicator more informative about future losses than the indicator it replaces. This book turns that principle into a complete, mathematically grounded toolkit. Organized around the seven Basel event types, from internal and external fraud to system failures and execution errors, it pairs each original model with a KRI set, the theorems that justify it, worked numerical examples and the controls that make it defensible to a regulator. Twenty-nine KV-enhanced models improve on the baselines, culminating in KV_Risk_Copula, a mixed-family vine copula that keeps both tails when losses are aggregated for capital. More than thirty real loss events, from Barings to Silicon Valley Bank and CrowdStrike, show which indicators bore on what happened.", "kw": "AI · KRIs · Basel event types · KV_Risk_Copula · Strategic & reputational risk"}, "option-pricing": {"t": "Kris Veeraghanta Option Pricing Theory", "s": "Beyond Black–Scholes", "d": "Black–Scholes assumes lognormal prices, constant volatility, continuous replication and a complete market. Markets have fat tails, volatility that clusters and rises after losses, and joint crashes. The Kris Veeraghanta Option Pricing Theory keeps the structure of Black–Scholes — prices are discounted expectations under a measure in which the forward is fair — and replaces every assumption. The theory unites the KV entropic measure, which prices downside and upside variance separately relative to a data-driven reference law, with the KV_Risk_Copula, a vine of Clayton, Gumbel, Clayton–Gumbel, Frank, Gaussian and Student-t edges. Its central result, the Kris Veeraghanta Option Pricing Theorem, proves existence, uniqueness and absence of arbitrage, reproduces every single-asset smile, introduces a new dependence premium, and nests Black–Scholes, Black-76, Garman–Kohlhagen, Bachelier, Margrabe and the Gaussian copula as special cases.", "kw": "Entropic measure · Vine copulas · CDS · CLOs · Swaptions · Historical evidence"}, "entropic": {"t": "The KV Entropic Option Pricing Model", "s": "Arbitrage-Free Derivative Pricing Beyond Black–Scholes", "d": "Black–Scholes prices an option as if returns were lognormal, volatility constant and trading continuous. None of this is true, and the volatility smile is the proof. The KV Entropic Option Pricing model keeps the one principle no model can do without, the absence of arbitrage, and replaces everything else with data. Its pricing measure is the one closest in relative entropy to the filtered historical law of returns that makes the forward fair and prices downside and upside variance separately. This book proves the KV Entropic Option Pricing Theorem, derives the call and the put formulas separately, shows that Black–Scholes and Bachelier are exact special cases, and applies the model to equity, commodity, futures, fixed-income, credit, foreign-exchange, digital, variance and multi-asset derivatives. For every product it asks the practical question: does entropic pricing beat Black–Scholes, and when does it not?", "kw": "Relative entropy · Put & call formulas · Equity · Commodities · Credit · FX"}, "decompose-pde": {"t": "Nonlinear Programming via Kronecker Decomposition", "s": "Theory, Algorithms, and the KV Decompose PDE Method", "d": "Every large nonlinear program eventually reduces to a linear system, and Gaussian elimination makes that step cost cubic time and quadratic memory. This book rebuilds the theory of nonlinear programming around a different idea: write the dominant operator as a Kronecker sum, diagonalize it exactly with a fast sine transform, and eliminate state and adjoint variables analytically. The optimality conditions, multipliers and duality stay classical. The computation does not: an exact direct solver for separable operators in O(N log N) work and O(N) memory, mesh-independent preconditioning, reduced-space elimination, and inexact Newton–Krylov methods with q-superlinear local convergence, compared against dense and sparse KKT solvers with every proof given two ways.", "kw": "PDE-constrained optimization · Kronecker sums · Newton–Krylov · Duality"}, "or-programming": {"t": "Operations Research Programming using Kronecker–Veeraghanta Theorem", "s": "LP · NLP · IP · MIP", "d": "Linear, nonlinear, integer and mixed-integer programs all spend their time in linear algebra. Interior-point methods, Karmarkar’s legacy, factor a new matrix at every step and, as this book proves, cannot keep the Kronecker structure a problem has. The Kronecker–Veeraghanta algorithm keeps its operator fixed: it decomposes the coupling once with Kronecker algebra and pushes every bound, penalty, nonconvexity and integrality condition into a separable step. The same decomposition then serves every iteration, restart and branch-and-bound node. Each problem class gets its own theory, its own case studies against the simplex method, Karmarkar’s algorithm, interior point and branch-and-cut, and an honest account of where the algorithm loses.", "kw": "Linear programming · Integer programming · Simplex · Karmarkar · Branch-and-cut"}, "game-theory": {"t": "Game Theory with Dynamic Rules and Artificial Intelligence", "s": "The Veeraghanta Space", "d": "Game theory asks what rational players will do when each one’s outcome depends on the others. Today many of the players are algorithms — pricing engines, bidding agents, poker and Go programs, trading systems — and the question is no longer only which equilibrium, but whether learning players reach one, how fast, and what they do instead. This book covers the major types of games, their players, outcomes and Nash equilibria, and the AI methods that now play them better than people do. It then introduces the Veeraghanta space: games whose rules change with the play — in the limit, a dynamic change in the rules with every move — and applies it to banks, traders and hedge funds facing regulators, dealers, clearing houses and prime brokers that rewrite the rules in response.", "kw": "Nash equilibrium · Self-play · Veeraghanta space · Hedge funds · Asset management"}, "country-risk": {"t": "Country Risk: Models, Theorems, Artificial Intelligence and Profitable Strategies", "s": "Default · Currency · Sanctions · AI", "d": "Russia defaulted on its ruble debt in 1998 and, with money in hand, on its dollar bonds in 2022. Iran’s rial has lost most of its value in a sequence of sanctions shocks, from 2012 to the war of 2026. This book turns those histories into a theory of country risk — sovereign default, currency collapse, convertibility, sanctions and contagion — with every model stated precisely and every theorem proved. It then shows asset managers, investors and hedge funds how to hedge country risk, how to size and time positions in it, and when the strategies and the financial products built on it — CDS, credit-linked notes, NDFs, currency options, political risk insurance, trade finance — are profitable. New in the third edition, Part VII covers copulas, vines, stress scenarios and U.S./EU regulation.", "kw": "Sovereign default · Sanctions · Russia · Iran · CDS · Copulas · AI early warning"}};
